        <div class="hero">
          <h1>Docs</h1>
          <p class="lede">snowglobe runs a Docker image where Docker isn't. It turns the image into a site, a folder of
            static files you can host anywhere. Open it in a browser tab, run it from one binary with no Docker
            installed, or give it to an agent as a sandbox. It starts in about a second, because it restores a
            snapshot taken once your app was ready.</p>
        </div>

        <section id="pages" aria-labelledby="pages-title">
          <h2 id="pages-title">Which page answers what</h2>
          <p class="intro">New here? Read <a href="#start">Getting started</a> below, then
            <a href="building/">Building a site</a>. Come back to this table when you have a question.</p>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="map">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Your question</th><th scope="col">Read</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>How do I install it and build my first site?</td><td><a href="#start">Getting started</a>, below</td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do I choose when the snapshot is taken, make the first load fast, or give the site more CPUs?</td><td><a href="building/">Building a site</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Can the machine reach the internet, a database, or my own machine?</td><td><a href="networking/">Networking</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do I run a site in a terminal, from a URL, or as one executable?</td><td><a href="running/">Running outside a browser</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How does an agent or a script run commands in a machine, by CLI, MCP or in a page?</td><td><a href="agents/">For agents and programs</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do I run many machines, services that scale to zero, or a whole fleet from one file?</td><td><a href="orchestration/">Orchestration and fleets</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do I notice a service's machine that hangs or answers with errors, and replace it?</td><td><a href="orchestration/#health">Health checks and healing</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do I restart what depends on something that failed, as an Elixir supervisor would?</td><td><a href="orchestration/#supervisors">Supervisors</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>How do machines reach each other?</td><td><a href="orchestration/#links">Links</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>What stops a guest from touching my computer?</td><td><a href="isolation/">Isolation</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Where does a run in the browser keep my work?</td><td><a href="saving/">Saving your work</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>What happens inside a build, and why did mine fail?</td><td><a href="how-it-works/">How it works</a></td></tr>
                <tr><td>What does this flag, command or variable do?</td><td><a href="reference/">CLI reference</a></td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Agents can read all of this as one text file: <a href="../llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
          <h2 id="start-title">Getting started</h2>
          <p class="intro">Build a site from a Dockerfile, preview it, then put the folder on any static host.</p>

          <h3 id="requirements">What you need</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Docker, or nothing.</strong> With Docker, snowglobe builds your Dockerfile and boots it in
              a build container. Without Docker, <code>--builder native</code> builds from an image in a registry
              or a saved archive. <code>snowglobe run --runtime native</code>, bundles and <code>snowglobe mcp</code>
              need no Docker either.</li>
            <li><strong>An arm64 Alpine image.</strong> Use <code>FROM alpine</code> (multi-arch; snowglobe builds it for
              arm64) or <code>FROM arm64v8/alpine</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>On an x86 host, QEMU's binfmt handlers</strong>, so Docker can build arm64 images. Docker
              Desktop has them. On Linux, run this once:
              <code>docker run --privileged --rm tonistiigi/binfmt --install arm64</code>.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 id="install">Install</h3>
          <p>One line installs the latest release into <code>~/.local/bin</code>. It works on Linux (x86-64 or
            arm64) and on a Mac with Apple silicon. It checks the download against the release's checksums and
            needs no sudo. You can <a href="../install.sh">read the script</a> first.</p>
          <pre>curl -fsSL <%= @site %>/install.sh | sh</pre>
          <p><code>SNOWGLOBE_VERSION=0.4.0</code> picks a version. <code>SNOWGLOBE_INSTALL_DIR</code> picks another
            directory.</p>
          <p>You can also download a binary from the
            <a href="https://github.com/filipecabaco/snowglobe/releases">releases</a>, or install with Go 1.27 or
            later:</p>
          <pre>go install github.com/filipecabaco/snowglobe/cmd/snowglobe@latest</pre>

          <h3 id="first-site">Your first site</h3>
          <p>Any directory with a Dockerfile, or any image reference, builds into <code>dist/</code>:</p>
          <pre>snowglobe build ./my-app          <span class="dim"># a directory with a Dockerfile, or an image reference</span>
snowglobe serve dist              <span class="dim"># preview at http://localhost:8000</span></pre>
          <p>The page shows a terminal running whatever the image runs: its <code>ENTRYPOINT</code> and
            <code>CMD</code>, with its <code>ENV</code> and <code>WORKDIR</code>.</p>
          <p><code>dist/</code> is plain static files. Upload it to GitHub Pages, a CDN or any web server. There is
            no snowglobe server.</p>
          <div class="note">
            <p>Everything runs on an emulated 64-bit ARM CPU, translated to WebAssembly as it runs, so expect it to
              be slower than native. Cold loads take seconds. Commands take tens of milliseconds to a few
              seconds.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 id="first-run">What the first build downloads</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>The tools image</strong>, the machine that boots and snapshots your image, from
              <code>ghcr.io/filipecabaco/snowglobe-tools</code>. A version that hasn't been released compiles it
              locally instead, once, in a few minutes.</li>
            <li><strong><code>snowglobe-vm</code></strong>, the machine that runs sites natively. A release of snowglobe
              is one file that carries its own and unpacks it into your cache the first time it runs a site
              natively. Built with <code>go install</code>, snowglobe downloads the release's instead.</li>
            <li><strong>The prebuilt kernel</strong>, once, when you build without Docker.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Releases are for Linux on x86-64 and arm64, and macOS on Apple silicon.</p>
        </section>
